1
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,170
About a million deaths
every year are attributable

2
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:12,030
to breathing fine particles
over just a few days.

3
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:16,410
That is the timescale on which a forecast
can let people protect themselves.

4
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:19,625
But what are fine particles?

5
00:00:19,995 --> 00:00:22,885
They are specks of soot,
smoke, dust and other matter,

6
00:00:22,935 --> 00:00:25,725
no wider than two and a half
thousandths of a millimetre:

7
00:00:25,775 --> 00:00:28,965
about thirty times
thinner than a human hair.

8
00:00:30,621 --> 00:00:34,291
They come from traffic,
industry, heating and fires,

9
00:00:34,341 --> 00:00:36,271
from desert dust and sea salt,

10
00:00:36,321 --> 00:00:39,991
and from gases, such as
volatile organic compounds,

11
00:00:40,061 --> 00:00:42,191
that turn into particles in the air.

12
00:00:45,588 --> 00:00:47,578
Breathed in, they reach
deep into the lungs,

13
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:49,878
and some can even enter the bloodstream.

14
00:00:52,288 --> 00:00:55,118
We count them in micrograms
per cubic metre of air.

15
00:00:55,168 --> 00:00:56,498
For a single day,

16
00:00:56,548 --> 00:01:00,758
the World Health Organization
advises staying below fifteen.

17
00:01:02,588 --> 00:01:03,978
And this pollution is local.

18
00:01:04,108 --> 00:01:06,838
It changes between a city
centre and its suburbs,

19
00:01:06,888 --> 00:01:09,238
along a valley, downwind of a fire.

20
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:13,497
Yet global forecasts are made on
grids about forty kilometres wide.

21
00:01:14,007 --> 00:01:16,617
Here is the same day,
seen at forty kilometres.

22
00:01:17,407 --> 00:01:19,297
The hotspots are flattened.

23
00:01:19,521 --> 00:01:21,711
But a grid this coarse does
not only blur the map.

24
00:01:21,761 --> 00:01:23,151
It hides people.

25
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:25,651
In twenty twenty-two,

26
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:30,231
even a perfect forty-kilometre
forecast would have missed

27
00:01:30,281 --> 00:01:33,711
a hundred and eighty-six
million people a day,

28
00:01:33,761 --> 00:01:35,211
breathing air above a World
Health Organization target.

29
00:01:37,055 --> 00:01:39,825
Where you live also decides
whether your air is measured.

30
00:01:40,754 --> 00:01:43,404
A study published in twenty
nineteen found that six

31
00:01:43,454 --> 00:01:47,024
countries in ten had no regular
monitoring of fine particles,

32
00:01:47,074 --> 00:01:49,224
and that people lived, on average,

33
00:01:49,794 --> 00:01:51,384
two hundred and twenty kilometres
from the nearest monitor.

34
00:01:53,188 --> 00:01:55,858
And coarse forecasts do
not miss everyone equally.

35
00:01:56,378 --> 00:02:01,118
In twenty twenty-two, a person in
Pakistan or Egypt spent, on average,

36
00:02:01,168 --> 00:02:02,898
more than three weeks in harmful air

37
00:02:02,948 --> 00:02:05,498
that a forty-kilometre
forecast could not see.

38
00:02:05,548 --> 00:02:09,218
In the United States or in
Germany, it was less than one day.

39
00:02:10,521 --> 00:02:13,271
Machine learning has
transformed weather forecasting.

40
00:02:13,321 --> 00:02:16,311
So why not forecast air
pollution at one kilometre?

41
00:02:17,141 --> 00:02:18,991
Two problems stand in the way.

42
00:02:20,254 --> 00:02:21,204
The first is scale.

43
00:02:21,254 --> 00:02:25,684
A continent at one kilometre is an
image of tens of millions of pixels,

44
00:02:25,734 --> 00:02:29,584
which is too large for most
networks to take in one piece.

45
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:31,850
So networks are applied tile by tile,

46
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,530
and they leave seams at
the edges of the tiles.

47
00:02:36,455 --> 00:02:37,585
The second is the peaks.

48
00:02:37,775 --> 00:02:40,285
Train a network to minimise
its squared error,

49
00:02:40,565 --> 00:02:43,525
and it predicts the average
of all the possible futures.

50
00:02:43,821 --> 00:02:45,991
The average is blurred,
and its peak is lower.

51
00:02:46,101 --> 00:02:49,351
The extremes that matter most
for health are smoothed away.

52
00:02:50,588 --> 00:02:52,098
And some peaks are not made locally.

53
00:02:52,228 --> 00:02:54,298
Dust, smoke and haze can arrive with

54
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:56,138
air that has travelled
thousands of kilometres.

55
00:02:57,821 --> 00:03:00,931
Our model, Foehn, is built
to address both problems.

56
00:03:00,981 --> 00:03:03,911
It forecasts a whole
continent in one pass,

57
00:03:03,961 --> 00:03:06,691
and joins these windows
without visible seams.

58
00:03:06,741 --> 00:03:08,111
It is generative:

59
00:03:08,321 --> 00:03:11,711
it draws possible futures
instead of their average,

60
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:14,231
and together they form an ensemble.

61
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:17,851
And it computes where the air
comes from, instead of learning it.

62
00:03:17,981 --> 00:03:19,651
Let's see how it works.

63
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,130
Let's take one question.

64
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:31,610
What will the air over Delhi
contain, five days from now?

65
00:03:31,855 --> 00:03:33,205
Start with the weather forecast:

66
00:03:33,275 --> 00:03:35,165
the wind that will carry the air there.

67
00:03:36,054 --> 00:03:37,324
Now, let's play it in reverse.

68
00:03:37,734 --> 00:03:40,744
A physics engine called
FLEXGREEN follows the

69
00:03:40,794 --> 00:03:43,624
air that will arrive
in Delhi back in time,

70
00:03:43,674 --> 00:03:45,244
to where it was five days earlier.

71
00:03:45,654 --> 00:03:48,464
Particles of air are followed
through the three-dimensional wind.

72
00:03:48,514 --> 00:03:50,424
The blue ones stay near the ground;

73
00:03:50,474 --> 00:03:52,204
the pink ones travel aloft.

74
00:03:52,388 --> 00:03:54,338
The same is done for every place on land.

75
00:03:54,448 --> 00:03:55,798
And none of this is learned:

76
00:03:55,848 --> 00:03:56,698
it is computed.

77
00:03:57,654 --> 00:03:59,984
How does FLEXGREEN know where the air was?

78
00:04:00,034 --> 00:04:02,584
Take the air above one
place on the forecast day.

79
00:04:02,634 --> 00:04:04,704
Where was it the day before,
and the days before that?

80
00:04:06,654 --> 00:04:10,264
FLEXGREEN takes the weather
forecast and runs it backwards.

81
00:04:10,314 --> 00:04:13,784
Every fifteen minutes, it moves a
particle of air against the wind,

82
00:04:13,834 --> 00:04:14,904
in three dimensions,

83
00:04:14,954 --> 00:04:17,764
with a small random kick for
the turbulence near the ground.

84
00:04:18,588 --> 00:04:21,958
It does this for thirty-two
particles over every place on land,

85
00:04:22,008 --> 00:04:24,438
released at random moments of the day.

86
00:04:24,488 --> 00:04:27,338
Together, they draw
where that air came from.

87
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:30,225
On the way, each particle fades:

88
00:04:30,355 --> 00:04:33,405
rain and the ground take
part of its aerosol away.

89
00:04:33,815 --> 00:04:36,085
What remains is its weight.

90
00:04:36,821 --> 00:04:40,331
Where the particles were, and their
weights, make a map for each place:

91
00:04:40,941 --> 00:04:44,171
its Green's function, named after
the mathematician George Green.

92
00:04:44,801 --> 00:04:48,251
Multiply the start day by this map,
and you get what the air brings there.

93
00:04:48,601 --> 00:04:52,151
The same map works for
pollution, smoke and emissions:

94
00:04:52,701 --> 00:04:57,191
computed once, with no learned
parameter, in about five minutes a day.

95
00:05:00,321 --> 00:05:03,151
Now, look at what that air
was carrying on the start day.

96
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:05,717
Each particle reads the
pollution where it was.

97
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:08,410
We keep the air that
started near the ground,

98
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:12,450
weight each reading by how much
of that air survived the journey,

99
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:13,200
and average.

100
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:14,610
Carried forward to Delhi,

101
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:18,110
that air brings about eighty-four
micrograms per cubic metre.

102
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:20,751
Done for every place, this gives a map:

103
00:05:20,801 --> 00:05:22,851
one of forty-four transport maps.

104
00:05:23,341 --> 00:05:25,751
Thirty-two others are
made from tracers that

105
00:05:25,801 --> 00:05:27,771
the network learns from the start day;

106
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:32,451
the rest are computed, such as the
emissions the air takes up on its way.

107
00:05:32,821 --> 00:05:34,631
Here is one of those learned tracers.

108
00:05:34,701 --> 00:05:36,991
The encoder makes it from the start day;

109
00:05:37,201 --> 00:05:38,751
nothing tells it what it should mean,

110
00:05:38,801 --> 00:05:40,671
it is learned together with the forecast.

111
00:05:40,981 --> 00:05:43,611
The same transport then
reads it where the air was.

112
00:05:43,761 --> 00:05:45,431
That is what the generator receives.

113
00:05:45,955 --> 00:05:46,725
Now, the generator.

114
00:05:46,915 --> 00:05:48,305
Take a single pixel: Delhi.

115
00:05:48,735 --> 00:05:51,825
The forecast does not start
from a guess, but from noise.

116
00:05:52,175 --> 00:05:55,305
Sixty-four random numbers, one
for each member of the ensemble.

117
00:05:55,788 --> 00:05:56,398
At each step,

118
00:05:56,448 --> 00:05:59,758
a neural network looks at the
current state and at the conditions,

119
00:05:59,808 --> 00:06:01,898
and tells it which way to move.

120
00:06:02,221 --> 00:06:04,551
In four steps, the noise
becomes a forecast.

121
00:06:10,221 --> 00:06:14,131
Sixty-four noise draws become
sixty-four forecasts: an ensemble.

122
00:06:14,181 --> 00:06:17,171
And the estimate made afterwards
falls right inside it.

123
00:06:17,421 --> 00:06:19,651
Now do the same for a
whole continent at once:

124
00:06:19,701 --> 00:06:22,891
twenty-nine million pixels, in
a single pass of the network.

125
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:26,930
Watch the noise fade, as the
kilometre-scale detail appears.

126
00:06:32,954 --> 00:06:33,984
So here is the whole machine.

127
00:06:34,374 --> 00:06:36,544
Computed transport tells
where the air came from.

128
00:06:36,594 --> 00:06:38,284
Learned tracers say what it carried.

129
00:06:38,334 --> 00:06:41,444
And the generator turns noise
into a one-kilometre forecast.

130
00:06:46,488 --> 00:06:47,218
Does it work?

131
00:06:47,328 --> 00:06:52,338
We compared its forecasts, issued
every second day of twenty twenty-two,

132
00:06:52,508 --> 00:06:54,718
with measurements at
more than six thousand

133
00:06:54,768 --> 00:06:56,118
stations that were never used to train it.

134
00:06:56,354 --> 00:06:59,584
Compared with the air usually found
in each place at that time of year,

135
00:06:59,634 --> 00:07:02,444
its error is about a
fifth lower one day ahead,

136
00:07:02,494 --> 00:07:04,844
and about a tenth lower four days ahead.

137
00:07:05,221 --> 00:07:08,011
Simply repeating today's measurement
does better for tomorrow,

138
00:07:08,061 --> 00:07:09,911
but only where a station exists.

139
00:07:10,301 --> 00:07:13,311
From two to four days
ahead, Foehn does better.

140
00:07:14,455 --> 00:07:15,845
Here is a real forecast at one kilometre,

141
00:07:16,435 --> 00:07:18,025
issued on the eighth of January,

142
00:07:18,075 --> 00:07:21,385
twenty twenty-two, over
the North China Plain.

143
00:07:21,435 --> 00:07:24,565
It saw the haze clear, day
after day, four days ahead.

144
00:07:28,155 --> 00:07:30,065
And it forecasts at the
scale of a kilometre,

145
00:07:30,155 --> 00:07:33,545
where the people a coarse
grid hides actually live.

146
00:07:35,521 --> 00:07:36,931
Compute what physics knows.

147
00:07:37,421 --> 00:07:38,551
Learn the rest.

148
00:07:38,941 --> 00:07:40,251
This is Foehn.
